INSERT INTO `posts` VALUES (3,'reel','Nice nice nice','Niceee','completed',NULL,'2026-11-19 07:52:40','2026-11-19 07:53:22',1,NULL),(6,'text','',NULL,'completed',NULL,'2026-11-19 08:27:52','2026-11-19 08:27:54',1,NULL),(8,'reel','APR: the yearly cost of borrowing, as a percentage. The number they hope you won\'t read. 🔍\nN$10,000 at 30% APR ≈ N$3,000+ a year if you pay nothing off. \"That\'s a lot of coffee.\" \"It\'s a lot of everything.\"\nCompare APRs, not adverts. Follow @whyamibroketoday for the next word.\nGeneral information, not financial advice.\n\n#apr #creditcard #loans #personalfinance #WhyAmIBrokeToday','What Is APR? | Penny\'s Dictionary','completed','2026-11-19 12:03:30','2026-11-19 11:58:07','2026-11-19 12:04:45',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(9,'video','10 years. N$250 million. And the bridge leads to a cow. 🐄\nPenny follows one \"1-year\" bridge from the promise to the ribbon, and shows who really pays for the delay. (Spoiler: you.)\nFull episode on YouTube, link in bio.\nGeneral information, not financial advice.\n\n#infrastructure #government #satire #moneyhumor #WhyAmIBrokeToday','Why Every Government Project Takes 10 Years','queued','2026-11-22 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:25:50','2026-11-19 17:25:50',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(10,'reel','10 years of waiting. Then the road just… stops. 🐄\n\"The road is phase two! Ready in just one year!\" Full episode on YouTube, link in bio.\n#infrastructure #government #satire #WhyAmIBrokeToday','The Bridge Took 10 Years. Look Where It Goes.','queued','2026-11-23 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:25:57','2026-11-19 17:25:57',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(11,'reel','Deficit: spending more than you take in. Sam: \"Hey!\" 🐷\nEarn N$100, spend N$120 = a N$20 deficit. All the old deficits added together = debt.\nWord #4 in Penny\'s Dictionary. Follow @whyamibroketoday for the next word.\n\n#deficit #debt #economics #moneyexplained #WhyAmIBrokeToday','What Is a Deficit? | Penny\'s Dictionary','queued','2026-11-24 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:26:04','2026-11-19 17:26:04',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(12,'reel','\"That\'s not really my money, right?\" Penny does the maths. 🧮\nOverrun: N$30 each. The delay: N$49,000 and 4½ months of Sam\'s life. Full episode on YouTube, link in bio.\nGeneral information, not financial advice.\n#infrastructure #commute #personalfinance #moneyhumor #WhyAmIBrokeToday','What a Late Project Really Costs You','queued','2026-11-25 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:26:09','2026-11-19 17:26:09',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(13,'reel','Compound interest: interest on your interest. A snowball. ❄️\nN$1,000 at 10% → N$1,100 → N$1,210 → N$1,331. Saving? It works for you. Borrowing? It rolls over you.\nWord #5 in Penny\'s Dictionary. Follow @whyamibroketoday for the next word.\nGeneral information, not financial advice.\n\n#compoundinterest #savings #investing #personalfinance #WhyAmIBrokeToday','Compound Interest Explained in 30 Seconds | Penny\'s Dictionary','queued','2026-11-26 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:26:16','2026-11-19 17:26:16',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(14,'reel','Who wins when a bridge runs 10 years late? Penny hands out the medals. 🥇\nHonourable mention: the cow. Full episode on YouTube, link in bio.\n#government #infrastructure #satire #moneyhumor #WhyAmIBrokeToday','Who Wins When a Project Runs 10 Years Late?','queued','2026-11-27 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:26:22','2026-11-19 17:26:22',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(15,'reel','Two foundation stones. No bridge. And now his face goes on the towers. 🏗️\nFull episode on YouTube, link in bio.\n#government #election #satire #WhyAmIBrokeToday','Why Does the Same Bridge Get Two Foundation Stones?','queued','2026-11-28 13:00:00','2026-11-19 17:26:28','2026-11-19 17:26:28',6,'mcp:claude-code'),(16,'text','A lot of what I ship still goes out the old way, a Linux VPS with PM2 and Nginx set up by hand, so Coolify caught my eye this week. It\'s an open-source, self-hostable alternative to Vercel, Heroku and Netlify, Apache-2.0 licensed, and you install it on your own server with one script. You point it at a GitHub repo or a Docker image and it handles deploys, PR previews, rollbacks, automatic HTTPS and scheduled database backups. It also ships a REST API, a CLI and an MCP server, which is the bit I\'d test first with Claude Code. I wouldn\'t move a client\'s production setup to it on day one, because one more layer on the box is one more thing to debug at 2am. For internal tools and SaaS demos at Xavia though, I\'d try it before paying for a hosted platform. Has anyone run it with more than a handful of apps on one server?',NULL,'completed','2026-11-21 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-21 05:00:01',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(17,'text','My default backend stack is boring on purpose, and I\'m fine with that. For most new projects it\'s NestJS, MySQL, Sequelize and Swagger. NestJS gives every developer on the team the same shape of module, controller and service, so a new person can find their way around on day one. MySQL is predictable and every hosting setup supports it. Sequelize isn\'t the trendiest ORM, but I know where it hurts and how to work around it, and that matters more to me than a nicer API. Swagger means the frontend team and the client can see the API contract without asking me for docs. When a project really needs more, like the bank work where we ran Kafka and Hazelcast, I add it for that problem only. Boring tools leave my thinking for the business logic, which is where clients actually pay us to be clever. I\'d rather debug a stack I know well on a bad day than one I picked because it was trending.',NULL,'queued','2026-11-24 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-20 14:56:08',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(18,'text','Anthropic released Claude Fable 5.2 and Claude Mythos 5.2 on November 9, and the line I keep thinking about is in their own announcement: for most everyday coding they still point you to Claude Opus 5.5, and keep Fable for the most ambitious, open-ended technical work. Fable 5.2 costs $8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output, and Mythos is the same model without the extra safeguards, only for vetted organizations. For a dev team, that\'s basically a two-tier setup. In our delivery process at Xavia, Claude Code writes the phased plan before anyone touches a ticket, and that planning step is where I\'d try the bigger model first. The tickets themselves can stay on the everyday model, where speed and cost matter more. I don\'t trust benchmark charts much until a model handles our own messy requirements, so that\'s the test I\'d run. Are you paying for the top model everywhere, or only where the hard thinking happens?',NULL,'queued','2026-11-26 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-20 14:56:08',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(19,'text','Passing a request ID through five layers of NestJS services just so your logs make sense is a waste of a parameter, and nestjs-cls fixes that. It\'s an MIT-licensed library that wraps Node\'s AsyncLocalStorage in a module that works with Nest\'s dependency injection, so any service can read request-scoped data without you threading it through every method. The obvious use is tracking a request ID for logging, which is exactly where I\'d start on a microservices setup with an API gateway and domain services. It also has a transactional plugin with a @Transactional decorator for Prisma, TypeORM, Knex and others. One honest note for my own stack: its docs say there won\'t be a Sequelize adapter, because Sequelize already has its own CLS-based transaction support. So I\'d use it for context and logging, and let Sequelize keep handling transactions. Small library, but it removes a kind of boilerplate that quietly spreads across a codebase.',NULL,'queued','2026-11-28 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-20 14:56:08',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(20,'text','When I built my own GitHub MCP server for Claude Code, writing the handlers was the easy part and writing the tool descriptions was the hard part. It\'s a TypeScript server with 13 tools, all around managing pull requests. The lesson I took away is that the model only knows what the name and the description tell it, so a vague description means it picks the wrong tool or calls two that overlap. What helps is treating each tool like a small API for a new teammate: one clear job, a name that says what it does, inputs with plain names, and a description that says when to use it and when not to. I also keep the output short and useful instead of dumping the whole GitHub response back, because the model has to read every line of it. Thirteen tools sounds like a lot, but each one stays small. If you\'re building an MCP server, spend more time on the descriptions than on the code, because that\'s the part the model actually reads.',NULL,'queued','2026-11-30 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-20 14:56:08',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(21,'text','Node.js 27 is now in alpha, with builds out on November 12 and 17, and it\'s the first line under the new schedule of one major release a year. The plan on the Node blog is simple: a new major every April, LTS every October, and no more odd and even split, so every release becomes LTS. The alpha builds aren\'t meant for production. They\'re for library authors and CI pipelines, and 27.0.0 itself is planned for April 2027. So my answer to upgrade now or wait is both. Production NestJS services should sit on a current LTS and stay patched, and the November 11 security releases for the 22, 24 and 26 lines are the kind you don\'t skip. The 27 alpha is something I\'d add as an extra job in CI, just to see what breaks early, and leave it there until April. Fewer majors a year is good news for a team like mine that looks after several client backends. Is anyone already running Node alphas in CI?',NULL,'queued','2026-12-03 05:00:00','2026-11-20 14:56:08','2026-11-20 14:56:08',1,'mcp:claude-code'),(22,'document','A Slack message is now enough to get a pull request started at Xavia. I built a small dev agent in TypeScript that picks up a task from Slack, uses the Anthropic API to work through the code, keeps Plane in the loop for tracking, and opens a PR on GitHub. It runs under PM2 on a plain server, nothing fancy. The hardest part was deciding where the agent stops. It stops at the PR, and a person reviews that PR like any other before anything gets merged. I also learned quickly that a vague Slack message gives you a vague PR, so the real skill is writing the request clearly in two or three lines. That habit has made our normal tickets better too, which I didn\'t expect. I wouldn\'t let an agent push straight to production, but for small fixes, having it write the first draft and open the PR is the kind of automation I actually trust.','My Slack message turns into a pull request','queued','2026-11-23 05:00:00','2026-11-20 18:11:57','2026-11-20 18:11:57',1,'mcp:claude-code');
